course: dictionary
lesson: 6268
title: A word from history: monastery
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A monastery is a building, or group of buildings, where monks live, pray and work together, following religious rules. Read the clues around the word. «In 793 Vikings raided the monastery of Lindisfarne in England» tells you a monastery is a place that can be attacked, and that it has a name, like a town or a castle.
explain: «After the fall of the West, monks in monasteries copied old books by hand.» Copied means made again, word for word. The monks did this work in monasteries, so a monastery was a place of learning as well as prayer.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «monks in a building where they pray copied old books by hand.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A monastery is a building, or group of buildings, where monks live, pray and work together.
fact: In the Middle Ages, monasteries were also centres of learning and farming, and places where books were copied by hand.
fact: «Lindisfarne» was a monastery in England, and Vikings raided it in 793.
fact: Monasteries exist in Christianity and in Buddhism.
fact: The plural of monastery is monasteries, so «Henry VIII closed many English monasteries» means he closed a lot of them.
exercise: «In 793 Vikings raided the monastery of Lindisfarne in England.» What is a monastery? | A ship that sails to foreign lands | A shop that sells old books | *A building where monks live, pray and work together | A king's palace with many servants
exercise: «After the fall of the West, monks in monasteries copied old books by hand.» Find and copy one word that means made again, word for word. | books | *copied | monks | monasteries
exercise: In the Middle Ages, what else was a monastery besides a place of prayer? | A castle with tall towers and guards | A place where ships were built | A market where people bought and sold books | *A centre of learning, farming and copying books
test: «The Primary Chronicle was probably put together at the Kyiv Caves Monastery.» What does this tell you about monasteries? | They were only places to sleep | They were a type of ship | *They were places where important writing could be made | They were always built on islands
test: Which religions have monasteries? | Only Buddhism | Only Christianity | No religion at all | *Christianity and Buddhism
exam: «Henry VIII closed many English monasteries.» What does closed mean here? | Made them bigger for more monks | Painted them bright colours | *Stopped them working as monasteries | Built a new road past them
